<!-- The wheel layout: the five commands sit in the segments of a ring,
     the way a game's weapon or radio wheel arranges its choices, each
     with its icon and its name; the middle keeps the chapter and the
     running time.

     The ring rests on a round panel in the colour the other layouts
     give their rectangular one, so the segments read as buttons on a
     panel rather than as shapes on the picture. The panel is one shape
     with the ring's hole leaving its middle showing, not a hub laid
     over it: two translucent shapes over each other double up their
     colour.

     Every position is worked out from one centre and one radius, so the
     ring stays a ring; the segments are five pre-rotated textures,
     because a skin cannot turn one texture five ways. Each segment
     comes in two weights - a faint one for the segments the remote is
     not sitting on and a solid one for the segment it is - the way the
     other layouts give their buttons a faint and a solid texture. That
     is what keeps the chosen colours readable: what the eye meets
     first is the panel in the panel colour, not a ring painted over
     it. The buttons draw nothing themselves - they are invisible hit
     areas over the segments, and focus is a swap of texture and colour
     on the segment, its icon and its name. The names carry a standing
     zoom: a wedge wants smaller writing than font10, and font10 is the
     smallest font name a skin can be relied on to define - the window
     is drawn with whichever skin is running, not with fonts of its
     own. Icons with text whatever the button style setting says:
     dialog.py prescribes it - which is also why there is no hint line
     under the ring. That line explains an unlabelled icon, and there
     are none here. -->
